course: dictionary
lesson: 3274
title: Erosion: the slow wearing away
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Erosion is the slow wearing away of rock, soil or land. Water, wind, ice and waves do the work bit by bit, often over many years. Look at the sentence: «Erosion is the carrying away of weathered bits of rock by water, wind, ice or gravity.» The words carrying away are your clue: erosion moves things from one place to another.
explain: Compare two words in «Breaking rock where it stands is called weathering; carrying the bits away is called erosion.» Weathering breaks rock where it stands. Erosion takes the broken bits away. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: does «carrying the bits away is called carrying the bits away» still make sense? Yes, so you have found the meaning.
explain: Erosion can also describe ideas, not just land. «The erosion of trust» means trust slowly getting weaker or being lost over time. The meaning is still slow wearing away, but now it is something you cannot touch.
fact: Erosion means the slow wearing away of rock, soil or land.
fact: Water, wind, ice and waves can all cause erosion.
fact: «Running water is the most powerful agent of erosion on Earth.» Rivers can carve valleys over time.
fact: Planting trees and grass helps to stop erosion, because roots hold the soil in place.
fact: Erosion can also mean the slow weakening or loss of something, like «the erosion of trust».
exercise: «Rising seas caused by global warming make coastal erosion and flooding worse.» What is coastal erosion? | *Land by the sea slowly worn away by waves | Sea water turning into ice | Fish swimming close to the shore | A huge wave that comes only once
exercise: «Breaking rock where it stands is called weathering; carrying the bits away is called erosion.» Which one is erosion? | Breaking rock where it stands | Growing plants on a hill | Freezing a lake in winter | *Carrying the bits of broken rock away
exercise: Which of these helps to stop erosion? | *Planting trees and grass | Letting the soil stay bare | Pouring water down a steep slope | Digging up all the plants
test: Wind blows dust away from a bare field, bit by bit. What is this called? | Flooding | *Wind erosion | Weathering, because the rock breaks | Freezing
test: Jess says, «The erosion of trust means trust got tighter.» What does erosion mean here? | Trust getting stronger and tighter | Trust shared with the sea | Trust that is made of rock | *Trust slowly weakening or being lost
exam: «Erosion is the carrying away of weathered bits of rock by water, wind, ice or gravity.» Which of these is an example of erosion? | A rock sitting still for a hundred years | A cloud drifting over a hill | A pond freezing solid in winter | *Waves wearing away a cliff bit by bit
